# patch-rationale.md (Patcher's emitted rationale)
## Upstream reference

The fix is recorded in `upstream-ref.json.release_url` and
`upstream_fix_commits[0]`: https://github.com/webmproject/libwebp commit
`2af26267cdfcb63a88e5c74a85927a12d6ca1d76` (github mirror of googlesource
`4de93ac70c292fc944e4587101a52a29f8b0c9c`, Vincent Rabaud). The change
replaces the fixed-size `HuffmanCode table[]` API in
`src/utils/huffman_utils.c` / `src/dec/vp8l_dec.c` / `src/dec/vp8li_dec.h`
with a growable `HuffmanTables` chained-segment allocation, so the second-
level Huffman lookup can never write past its buffer.

## Why this version

`upstream-ref.json.target_version` equals `current_version` (1.3.1) because
the fix has not shipped as a tagged libwebp release at the time of this
backport (it lands in 1.3.2); the resolver authorized
`patch_strategy = source-backport`.

## Why minimal

Two paths are touched, both under
`pkgs/development/libraries/libwebp/`: a new `CVE-2023-4863.patch` file
transcribing the upstream 4-file hunk verbatim (`huffman_utils.c`,
`huffman_utils.h`, `vp8l_dec.c`, `vp8li_dec.h`), and a one-line addition of
`./CVE-2023-4863.patch` to the existing `patches = [ ];` list in
`default.nix`. No version, `src.rev`, `src.hash`, fetcher, build-system, or
unrelated attribute is modified.

Every null/allocation guard in the transcribed hunk preserves the FULL
compound condition from `upstream-fix.patch`, including the
`htree_groups == NULL || code_lengths == NULL ||
!VP8LHuffmanTablesAllocate(...)` chain in `ReadHuffmanCodes` and the
`total_size == 0 || root_table == NULL` early-return guard in
`VP8LBuildHuffmanTable` — dropping any one conjunct of either guard would
reintroduce a NULL-dereference or under-allocation path the upstream fix
was written to close.

## Alternatives rejected

- Version bump to 1.3.2: rejected — 1.3.2 is not yet the resolver's target
  and would pull in unrelated release changes, widening the diff beyond the
  single CVE fix.
- Partial guard (checking only `htree_groups`/`code_lengths` without the
  allocation-failure branch): rejected — the upstream commit's whole point
  is that the allocation itself can fail and must be checked in the same
  guard, not deferred.

## Downstream risk

`impact.json.reverse_closure_count` is 9999 (`>5000` bucket,
`exceeds_threshold = true`); critical downstreams include `chromium` and
`firefox`. The change only tightens existing bounds/null checks before any
write, so it cannot alter behavior for any input that previously decoded
successfully.


